3|0@asoiaf^1440889334#cuk6bc7:17-40$:Somebody who works with steel once used a winemaking analogy. It would be like if we lost all our winemaking knowledge, with the exception of, "wine is made from fermented grapes." We wouldn't know what variety of grapes makes which type of wine. We wouldn't know anything about the crucial soils the grapes grow in. We wouldn't know how they used to balance the tannin, and a host of other factors. All of this can be easily rediscovered to make great wines, but they likely would taste different from the original recipes. I'm of the opinion that modern Damascus steel is probably better than the old stuff. It was just better than alternatives people had at the time, so it got a reputation. Also, it's pretty. People like pretty things.
